WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:14.752
My name's Lynn Wright.

00:00:14.752 --> 00:00:17.670
I'm customer service team leader,

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:21.000
and I manage repairs and reproofs.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.779
I'm now going to walk you through

00:00:22.779 --> 00:00:25.940
how our team restores a Beauchamp jacket.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:27.730
It's an older-style jacket.

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:30.530
We don't do this jacket as much now.

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:33.210
I think it was made in 2005.

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:36.180
The jacket has a rip on the
right side of the front,

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.860
and the button needs replaced.

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:40.670
The jacket looks like it's dried out

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:43.490
and it hasn't been rewaxed for years.

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:44.656
Barbour jackets are waxed

00:00:44.656 --> 00:00:47.272
so that they are water-resistant.

00:00:47.272 --> 00:00:49.120
Over time, the wax will wear off,

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:50.980
so we recommend that they are sent back

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:54.896
to be rewaxed every year to
keep it resistant to water.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:01.630
Firstly, I'm going to give
it to one of me operators.

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:05.780
Julie-Ann is given a job sheet
to go along with the jacket.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:08.880
And we'll take the old button off.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.860
We use an unpick. An
unpick's a little tool

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:14.320
that you just carefully
unpick the threads,

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.362
and it loosens the button off.

00:01:17.746 --> 00:01:21.330
And we'll measure the fabric
out for the size of the patch.

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:25.470
And then we'll fold the patch

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:29.070
and we'll actually mark around
the area where it's ripped.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:32.270
We cut the patch a bit
bigger, about a centimeter,

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:37.103
just in case as we're repairing
the jacket it does rip more.

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:43.390
So, we'll mark it.

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:45.810
We'll place the jacket in the machine,

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:48.450
and we'll edge-stitch the patch

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.367
where we've done the marking.

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:58.810
The old button has a crack in it,

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:01.740
so we're replacing it
with a brand-new button.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.970
We'd sew that on,
hand-sewn, with a needle.

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:07.813
There's normally four holes in the button,

00:02:07.813 --> 00:02:11.170
so we'll go from each corner,
crisscrossing the thread,

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:14.790
and then we'd wind the
thread round the button

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:19.083
to secure the button and then
snip it off with our clippers.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:21.895
We now check for quality,

00:02:21.895 --> 00:02:23.295
and we'll hang it on the rail,

00:02:23.295 --> 00:02:25.707
ready for midline inspection.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.140
I'm going to take it over to Neil,

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:33.665
and he will rewax the jacket
and bring it back to life.

00:02:34.665 --> 00:02:36.980
Firstly, Neil will pick up a scoop

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:40.520
and fill it with hard wax from the drum

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:42.913
and place it into the heated pot.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:45.670
The wax is melted at a temperature

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:47.480
of no more than 60 degrees.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:49.210
Otherwise, it will be too hot

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:51.152
and it will go through to the lining.

00:02:52.459 --> 00:02:54.700
We remove the garment from the rail

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:57.311
and position it onto the table.

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:02.670
We will apply some fabric into the jacket

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:04.503
to protect the lining.

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:14.330
He'll now zip the jacket
up and fasten any buttons

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:17.223
or press studs on the front of the jacket.

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:22.840
Neil is now ready to
start the rewax process,

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:24.631
which, he'll use a sponge

00:03:24.631 --> 00:03:28.956
to apply the heated wax round
the parts of the jacket,

00:03:28.956 --> 00:03:31.637
using a consistent approach.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:38.218
Neil uses long gliding strokes

00:03:38.218 --> 00:03:39.893
for the application of the wax.

00:03:39.893 --> 00:03:41.834
It gives it a better finish.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:46.730
As Neil is applying the wax,

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:49.093
you'll see that he doesn't touch

00:03:49.093 --> 00:03:51.243
the new fabric on the repair,

00:03:51.243 --> 00:03:54.160
as that's already got a lot of wax in it.

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:01.800
The rewax table is also
heated at 60 degrees.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.950
So the combination of the heated wax

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:08.260
and the heated table helps blend the wax

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:10.593
into the jacket to bring it back to life.

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:15.280
As you can see, Neil has
now done half the jacket,

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:16.920
and you can see the difference

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:18.720
of when the jacket's reproofed

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.033
and when it hasn't been reproofed.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:26.160
Neil has now finished the wax-on process

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:29.760
and will now begin the wax-off process.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:31.937
Neil will use a dry cloth

00:04:31.937 --> 00:04:35.435
to take any excess wax off the jacket

00:04:35.435 --> 00:04:39.127
around the seams, studs, and the buttons.

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:43.640
Finally, Neil will unzip the jacket

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:46.118
and remove any fabric he's put in

00:04:46.118 --> 00:04:48.240
to protect the lining,

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:52.143
check the lining is clean
and is in perfect condition.

00:04:53.750 --> 00:04:56.350
When the rewax process is finished,

00:04:56.350 --> 00:05:00.085
we do allow the jacket
to be hung up and dried

00:05:00.085 --> 00:05:03.251
for anything from 30 minutes to an hour

00:05:03.251 --> 00:05:05.026
before we dispatch.

00:05:18.228 --> 00:05:20.780
The jacket will now be
sent out to the customer,

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:23.770
who will be very happy with
the outcome of a jacket

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:27.143
that's been restored,
repaired, and rewaxed,

00:05:27.143 --> 00:05:29.467
and they'll be able to
wear it for years to come

00:05:29.467 --> 00:05:31.800
on their adventures in the outdoors.

